Erm... from the weapons masters, smiths, and researchers I've spoken
to, and books I've read, that's not the case. A long sword is a generic
term for a one-handed weapon double-edged blade; it is meant for
martial purposes and therefore is longer and stronger than the rapier
but still balanced to be wielded in one hand. It is _not_ quite useful
as a two handed weapon (though could be wielded two handed by a smaller
person, I suppose). A bastard sword, also called a hand-and-a-half
sword, is longer than a long sword with a slightly thicker blade (but
similarly designed as a long sword); a skilled and strong warrior could
wield this blade either one handed or two handed (as I have seen
demonstrated with a real bastard sword). A true "two-handed sword" was
historically called a great sword. A larger version of the great sword
was used in Scotland and is very long--that is a claymore.

There are further variations on the theme of swords of course... but
that's the general gist of the english/west european swords at least. I
am not an expert on the subject by any means, but I've consistently
found the above information in a number of places or talking to people
who not only have researched them but can make or wield them. Some
inconsistencies are bound to occur with varying interpretations of
history, etc.

The old D&D terms are sometimes a bit misleading (the newer edition of
D&D has somewhat improved them, but Nethack uses the older
descriptions, so that point is moot), but I find it's easiest not to
think of D&D (or Nethack) weapons in terms of what they compare to in
history but simply about them as terms for what they do. So "long
sword" in that other world of gaming equals pointy object that does d8
of damage... and I try not to worry much more about it. :)
